v2-bootstrap: state the locator's ordinal and ancestry facts exactly (PATCH 7)

Comment-only, one file (dag/gunbc/parse_verdict_restore.dag), 0 non-comment lines
changed. Production behaviour, controls and the emitted plan are untouched; PATCH 6
(56ead5acee3, tree fa9c77c01883) is preserved as the reviewed baseline.

The locator comment claimed the candidates are "always ancestors of the start and
never the start" and that ordinal 0 is reachable "which no caller asks for". Both
are false: data parse_verdict_restore_ancestor_ordinals is [0..11] and the emitted
step runs `git rev-list --first-parent --skip=0 --max-count=1 <start>`, which
returns the start itself. Exclusion of the judged commit is not enforced by this
code; it is a consequence of which commit each route names as its start.

The comment now states the selection facts: ordinal 0 is the start and 1..11 are
its first-parent predecessors; the start is github.event.pull_request.base.sha on a
pull_request and github.event.before otherwise, so merge_group and
workflow_dispatch name no start and every ordinal is a typed miss (a full walk). It
drops the blanket ancestry claim -- the job checks out github.sha, GitHub's merge
commit for the pull_request triggers, distinct from the source head
github.event.pull_request.head.sha, and nothing requires the reported base to be an
ancestor of either -- and records that a force push can make `before` a non-ancestor
while a batched push neither makes the two equal nor by itself fails anything.

The standing distinction is retained and moved up front: standing is decided per
member by gunbc.parse_verdict_schema.parse_verdict_standing from the head's and the
base's parse-compiler identity and the digest THIS walk recomputed from the bytes on
disk; an ancestor's position in the list confers nothing. No claim is made that any
of these event shapes has been executed: the paragraph says it is a reading of the
workflow source and the emitted YAML.
